    {
        "id":"undertell_book",
        "iconID":"items_newb:551",
        "name":"Undertell: Its Ghosts and History",
        "displaytype":"quest",
        "category":"other",
        "description":"Undertell: Its Ghosts and History\n\nFew places in Dhayavar inspire as much dread and fascination as the caverns beneath Mt. Galmore. Known collectively as Undertell, these depths have claimed the lives of miners, soldiers, scholars, and fortune-seekers alike.\n\nWhat follows is a collected account of the known and suspected truths of Undertell, compiled from fragmented records, hearsay, and personal investigation.\n\nChapter I: The Mines Beneath the Mountain\n\nIt is beyond dispute that Undertell was once heavily mined. Gold veins, rare gems, and other valuable stones were extracted here for decades. Even now, abandoned tunnels and collapsed shafts bear unmistakable marks of pick and flame.\n\nThough the mines were eventually sealed, it is widely believed that much wealth remains undisturbed in the deeper reaches. Fear, not scarcity, ended the mining operations.\n\nThose brave or foolish enough to venture below still return with traces of gold, confirming that the mountain has not yet given up all it holds.\n\nChapter II: Miner's Sickness\n\nThose who labored in the caverns beneath Undertell spoke of a sickness that came not from the air or the water, but from the stones themselves. At first, it was subtle. Miners complained of a bitter taste that lingered on the tongue after long hours in the tunnels. Their breath grew sour, and their sleep was broken by strange dreams of distant fires, of voices echoing through stone, of light burning behind closed eyes.\n\nThe sickness did not strike all who entered Undertell, only those who worked near the deeper seams, where the heartstone grew thickest in the rock. Those who merely passed through the caverns did not feel its touch. Those who lingered, who carved it free with bare hands and iron tools, carried something back with them when they returned to the surface. Not a curse.\n\nChapter III: On Ghosts and Lingering Spirits\n\nMany claim Undertell is haunted. Travelers speak of pale figures watching from afar, whispers echoing through stone and shapes that vanish when approached.\n\nSome insist these are the dead miners, bound by unfinished labor. Others claim the spirits are something older, drawn to the mountain long before any pick struck rock.\n\nThere is no agreement on whether these ghosts are hostile. Accounts vary wildly, some explorers report being ignored entirely, while others describe sudden violence without warning.\n\nChapter IV: The Lethgar and the Elytharans\n\nTwo distinct groups of spirits are frequently mentioned in surviving accounts.\n\nThe Lethgar are described as orderly, almost disciplined, haunting areas closest to the old mining routes. Some believe they were guards or overseers in life.\n\nThe Elytharans, by contrast, appear scattered and erratic. Their presence is strongest near collapsed tunnels and forgotten chambers. Whether they are miners, prisoners, or something else entirely remains unclear.\n\nIt is possible these distinctions are imagined by fearful minds seeking patterns where none exist.\n\nChapter V: Of Darkness and the Shadow\n\nMany who venture into Undertell report an oppressive presence - a weight that presses upon the mind and spirit alike. Some describe it as the Shadow itself, seeping from the stone.\n\nOthers argue this sensation is merely the result of isolation, darkness, and lingering fear passed down through generations.\n\nThere is no proof that Undertell is the source of the Shadow, only that the two are often spoken of together.\n\nChapter VI: Creatures of the Deep\n\nBeyond spirits, there are reports of creatures inhabiting the lower caverns. Some resemble twisted men, others living shadows given form.\n\nWhether these beings are monsters, spirits, or something else entirely is a matter of debate. Few witnesses survive long enough to offer clear descriptions.\n\nStill, enough similar accounts exist to suggest that Undertell is not empty and that something watches those who descend too far.\n\nChapter VII: A Warning to the Curious\n\nUndertell rewards greed as often as it punishes it. For every tale of riches recovered, there are three of those who never returned.\n\nIf knowledge is sought, tread carefully. If wealth is desired, expect to pay for it.\n\n[the remaining pages are soaked in blood and thus unreadable.]"
    },
